## 🔤 Naming Conventions

### General Rules

```swift
// ✅ Good: Clear, descriptive names
func fetchCountryDetails(for code: String) async throws -> CountryDetail
var isLoadingCountries: Bool
let maximumRetryCount = 3

// ✅ Good: Use verb phrases for functions/methods
func calculateDistance()
func updateUserInterface()
func validateInput()

// ✅ Good: Use noun phrases for properties/variables
var userSettings: UserSettings
let countryList: [Country]

// ❌ Bad: Abbreviations and unclear names
func fcd(c: String) async throws -> CD // What does this do?
var lc: Bool // What is this?
let mrc = 3 // What does mrc mean?
```

### Swift API Design Guidelines

```swift
// ✅ Good: Omit needless words
func remove(_ element: Element) // Not removeElement
func insert(_ element: Element, at index: Int)

// ✅ Good: Name according to side effects
func sorted() -> [Element] // No side effects - returns new
mutating func sort() // Side effects - modifies in place

// ✅ Good: Boolean properties read as assertions
var isEmpty: Bool
var isLoading: Bool
var hasValidData: Bool

// ✅ Good: Protocols that describe capabilities end in -able, -ible
protocol Loadable { }
protocol Cancellable { }
protocol Codable { }
```

### Case Conventions

```swift
// ✅ Good: camelCase for properties, methods, variables
var countryName: String
func loadCountries() { }

// ✅ Good: PascalCase for types (classes, structs, enums, protocols)
struct Country { }
class CountryService { }
enum LoadingState { }
protocol CountryServiceProtocol { }

// ✅ Good: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for static constants (optional)
static let MAXIMUM_RETRY_COUNT = 3

// ✅ Good: lowerCase for enum cases
enum NetworkError {
    case notConnected
    case timeout
    case invalidResponse
}
```

